# Two parts of one composed layer, drawn from one filing, with what each contributes. One row per
# ordered pair of part layers that the same fusion takes from the same part filing, carrying each
# side's conversion factor.
#
# The self-join inside one fusion
#   Two self-joins of composition/parts.sqlc live in these queries, and they answer opposite
#   questions:
#     here                       joined on the composed layer: two parts inside one total
#     eliminations/derived.sqlc  joined on the part: one part inside two totals
#   The first is a fusion's internal structure, which is what an `asrt:Elimination` speaks about:
#   its `between` names which of its own parts overlap. The second is two documents reaching for
#   the same supply, which is a referral to a person. Same operands, a different key, and making
#   them one would be a category error.
#
# Ordered, because its caller needs the order
#   A coupling has a direction, `from` and `to`, so reports/coupling_absorbed_by_a_fusion.sqlc must
#   be able to look up the pair the coupling names in the order the coupling names it. Its sibling
#   eliminations/derived.sqlc keeps each pair once with the row comparison `<` instead, because an
#   overlap has no direction, and reporting it twice would be reporting it twice.
#
# A layer paired with itself is kept on purpose
#   A layer is trivially a sibling of itself, and the caller wants that row: a coupling whose ends
#   are the same layer is ruled out by pm.coupling's own key, so excluding it here would guard
#   against something the schema already forbids while quietly changing what a future caller sees.
-- composition/parts.sqlc crossed with itself on the composed layer and the part filing.
SELECT x.composition, x.composed_layer,
       x.part_filing,
       x.part_layer AS from_layer,
       y.part_layer AS to_layer,
       x.factor_low  AS from_factor_low,  x.factor_mode AS from_factor_mode,
       y.factor_low  AS to_factor_low,    y.factor_mode AS to_factor_mode
FROM (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) x
JOIN (
    :compose(composition/parts.sqlc)
) y
  ON y.composition    = x.composition
 AND y.composed_layer = x.composed_layer
 AND y.part_filing    = x.part_filing
